select public.prepify_load_bank('Chemistry', 3, $j$[["When an electron moves from a lower energy shell to a higher energy shell, what is the underlying physical requirement?",["The electron must emit a photon.","The electron must absorb a specific amount of energy.","The nucleus must decrease its proton count.","The atom must become an ion."],"B",3,0,"b17a72f4053e"],["Which observation from Rutherford's experiment suggested that most of the atom is empty space?",["Most alpha particles passed straight through the foil.","Most alpha particles were deflected.","Gold foil melted during the experiment.","Electrons were emitted from the foil."],"A",3,1,"a5ef2266dfbe"],["An atom contains 17 protons and 17 electrons. Its net charge is:",["+17","+34","-17","0"],"D",3,0,"4f187d95dae6"],["Which particle contributes the least to the mass of an atom?",["Proton","Neutron","Electron","Nucleus"],"C",3,0,"bb4ddbe6b3f7"],["Why is it impossible to map the exact trajectory of an electron around the nucleus?",["Electrons move too slowly to be tracked.","Electrons behave as quantum particles with probabilistic paths rather than fixed orbits.","The nucleus is too small to serve as a reference point.","Electrons have no mass."],"B",3,0,"7e627440a083"],["Which force is primarily responsible for keeping protons and neutrons bound together within the nucleus?",["Gravitational force","Electromagnetic force","Strong nuclear force","Centripetal force"],"C",3,0,"814491823530"],["Which statement best supports the existence of a dense nucleus?",["Alpha particles were strongly deflected.","Electrons have negative charge.","Atoms combine to form compounds.","Neutrons are neutral."],"A",3,0,"f35efc959999"],["Which scientist discovered the electron?",["Rutherford","Chadwick","Bohr","Thomson"],"D",3,0,"af15e1f36f60"],["If an atom undergoes a radioactive change that alters its proton number, what is the direct consequence?",["The atom's identity changes to a different element.","The atom becomes a molecule.","The number of electrons remains unchanged.","The mass of the atom remains identical."],"A",3,1,"f1aa5e3c6161"],["How does the energy level of an electron shell correlate with its distance from the nucleus?",["Larger distance implies lower energy.","Distance has no relationship with energy.","Larger distance implies higher energy.","Distance is inversely proportional to the square of the energy."],"C",3,0,"d8ce2bd7b6f0"],["The atomic model is best described as:",["A perfect replica of the atom.","A tool to aid our understanding of complex subatomic structure.","A set of unchangeable laws.","A physical scale model that shows the actual size of protons."],"B",3,0,"49e5fe8d7a68"],["If a nucleus loses a neutron but the number of protons remains the same, the atom becomes:",["A different element.","A negatively charged ion.","A positively charged ion.","An isotope of the same element."],"D",3,1,"2eb82f3a996d"],["An atom's identity is based on 8 protons. If you add 2 neutrons, it is still oxygen. Why?",["Neutrons do not determine the element's identity.","Protons determine the identity, and that number stayed 8.","The nucleus is too large to change.","The mass number is irrelevant."],"B",3,0,"25345c5fb1c4"],["The second shell can accommodate a maximum of:",["2 electrons","4 electrons","8 electrons","18 electrons"],"C",3,0,"4c9dfbed9d8d"],["Which atomic model first introduced the concept of quantized energy levels?",["Dalton's model","Thomson's model","Rutherford's model","Bohr's model"],"D",3,0,"719037d21077"],["Which experiment led to the rejection of Thomson's plum pudding model?",["Gold foil experiment","Cathode ray experiment","Oil drop experiment","Radioactivity experiment"],"A",3,1,"0f2fa1ef9eda"],["According to modern atomic theory, the exact position of an electron:",["Can always be determined precisely","Is fixed in a circular orbit","Cannot be known with complete certainty","Is inside the nucleus"],"C",3,0,"8714cd45717a"],["The discovery of the neutron was made by:",["Dalton","Chadwick","Bohr","Rutherford"],"B",3,0,"2c63dfd7465b"],["When interpreting the relationship between a particle's charge and its path in an electric field, we observe:",["Neutral particles are deflected the most.","Oppositely charged particles deflect in opposite directions.","Both positive and negative particles move toward the same plate.","Only electrons can be deflected."],"B",3,0,"c84b3ccb2bff"],["What happens to the average distance of an electron from the nucleus as the shell energy level increases?",["It decreases.","It increases.","It remains the same.","It becomes zero."],"B",3,0,"c30b2d0cd96e"],["The nucleus occupies:",["Most of the volume of the atom","Half of the atom","Entire atom","Very little space inside the atom"],"D",3,0,"9ed33bdf1811"],["How would an electron react if exposed to a uniform electric field?",["It would be attracted to the negative electrode.","It would be attracted to the positive electrode.","It would remain stationary.","It would only move if it were a proton."],"B",3,0,"559e0310b2eb"],["Which scientist first proposed that atoms are indivisible particles?",["Dalton","Thomson","Rutherford","Bohr"],"A",3,0,"7b1344020967"],["In Thomson's model, electrons were assumed to be:",["Revolving around the nucleus","Embedded in a positively charged sphere","Located in fixed shells","Present inside neutrons"],"B",3,0,"f8bb40fd293c"],["How does an alpha particle behave in a magnetic or electric field compared to an electron?",["They behave identically because they are both subatomic.","The alpha particle deflects much less due to its significantly higher mass and positive charge.","The alpha particle is unaffected by electric fields.","The electron is deflected less because it is lighter."],"B",3,0,"3b44cb69d0c4"],["What is the structural role of neutrons in a nucleus containing multiple protons?",["They act as a glue to overcome the electrostatic repulsion between positively charged protons.","They increase the volume of the atom.","They provide energy for the electrons.","They define the chemical properties of the element."],"A",3,0,"3b1e352799ad"],["Why is the concept of a \"shell\" as an energy level more accurate than the concept of an \"orbit\"?",["Shells are physical rings made of matter.","Orbits are only for planets, not atoms.","Shells are bigger than orbits.","Orbits imply a fixed, predictable path, whereas shells define specific energy states where electrons exist based on probability."],"D",3,0,"1b73e17ddb0a"],["Why are electrons described as quantum particles?",["Because they have a large mass.","Because they are found only in the nucleus.","Because they exhibit behavior that classical mechanics cannot explain.","Because they move in straight lines."],"C",3,0,"cfca00e1bfb1"],["An atom contains 18 electrons arranged as 2, 8, 8. The total number of protons is:",["8","16","18","20"],"C",3,0,"a8036d3634ca"],["An atom has a nucleon number of 23 and an atomic number of 11. How many neutrons are in the nucleus?",["11","12","23","34"],"B",3,0,"477168333c57"],["How does the 'strong nuclear force' overcome the electrostatic repulsion between protons in a high-atomic-number nucleus?",["It acts at long ranges to pull protons toward the electron cloud.","It is an attractive force that dominates at extremely short distances, counteracting electrostatic repulsion.","It transforms protons into neutrons.","It is a gravitational effect generated by the nucleus mass."],"B",3,0,"d7f110ca8693"],["If an atom has a nucleon number (A) and an atomic number (Z), why does the loss of a neutron change the isotope but not the chemical identity?",["Because chemical identity is governed solely by electron configuration, which is determined by proton count.","Because neutrons carry a positive charge.","Because neutrons have zero mass.","Because the nucleus expands."],"A",3,1,"0753cd6034c2"],["A student says, \"Rutherford's model proved that electrons are present in the nucleus.\" Which fact directly disproves this statement?",["Most α-particles passed through the foil.","Electrons were discovered before Rutherford's experiment.","Rutherford proposed that electrons move around the nucleus.","Gold foil is a metal."],"C",3,1,"f35f4e9fb015"],["Which feature of the atom remained unchanged from Rutherford's model to Bohr's model?",["Electrons carrying a positive charge","Electrons embedded in positive charge","Absence of nucleus","Presence of a central nucleus"],"D",3,0,"cfcbfa5e9f60"],["If Rutherford had observed all α-particles passing straight through the foil without any deflection, he would most likely have concluded that:",["Atoms contain no dense central region.","Positive charge is concentrated in the nucleus.","Electrons are negatively charged.","Bohr's model is correct."],"A",3,0,"f50b5165d65c"],["If you have a nucleus with 20 protons and 20 neutrons, what happens to the electrostatic repulsion if one proton is added?",["The repulsion decreases.","The atom becomes a gas.","The repulsion increases, requiring more neutrons for nuclear stability.","The number of electrons stays the same."],"C",3,0,"85218dd01991"],["Why is the shell model considered an \"aid\" rather than a photographic representation?",["Because atoms are too small to have a structure.","Because it simplifies the complex quantum reality of electron distribution for educational clarity.","Because atoms do not have shells.","Because shells are only visible under high heat."],"B",3,0,"ea6d449db547"],["Bohr's model successfully explained hydrogen spectra because it assumed that electrons:",["Have no mass.","Could possess any energy.","Remain inside the nucleus.","Occupy specific energy levels."],"D",3,0,"30d5f525648e"],["An atom has atomic number Z and mass number A. Which expression represents the number of neutrons?",["Z − A","A/Z","A − Z","Z + A"],"C",3,0,"46d1d0bfa7eb"],["Dalton's atomic theory could not explain isotopes because it assumed:",["Atoms contain electrons.","Atoms of the same element are identical in mass.","Atoms contain nuclei.","Atoms are mostly empty space."],"B",3,0,"d52cfc4e002b"],["Which conclusion can be drawn from the fact that α-particles are positively charged?",["They are repelled by the positively charged nucleus.","They are attracted toward the nucleus.","They are neutral.","They contain electrons."],"A",3,0,"19ba4b1aa643"],["Which statement would be impossible?",["Two isotopes having different mass numbers","Two isotopes having different neutrons","Two isotopes having the same chemical properties","Two isotopes having different atomic numbers"],"D",3,0,"a97d7e31b49a"],["Relative to a proton, the mass of an electron is approximately:",["Equal","1/1836th","Double","10 times greater"],"B",3,0,"44264e75721f"],["The existence of ions demonstrates that:",["Atoms can gain or lose electrons.","Atoms can gain or lose protons easily.","Atomic number changes during chemical reactions.","Nuclei are unstable."],"A",3,0,"08b0653d4cd0"],["Why can we not map the \"exact path\" of an electron?",["Because it isn't moving.","Because the act of measuring its position affects its momentum.","Because electrons are too heavy.","Because the nucleus blocks our view."],"B",3,0,"f80df6171a0b"],["How does the periodic table arrange elements based on atomic structure?",["By mass, regardless of proton count.","By increasing atomic number.","By the number of neutrons.","By the total volume of the atom."],"B",3,0,"889d873b6d0a"],["In an electric field, why does a neutron travel completely straight regardless of its velocity?",["It is repelled by both plates equally.","It is moving too fast for the field to affect it.","Its mass is too large.","It lacks an electric charge, so it experiences no electrostatic force."],"D",3,0,"cfe19fb153ba"],["In an atom, the \"empty space\" is:",["The region occupied by the electron shells.","Where the nucleus is located.","A myth; there is no empty space.","Filled with protons."],"A",3,0,"100d3307329d"],["How does the energy requirement for removing an electron from the first shell compare to the third shell?",["It is easier to remove from the first shell.","They are identical.","It is much harder to remove from the first shell because it is closer to the nucleus.","Electrons cannot be removed from the first shell."],"C",3,0,"4ee6f8958294"],["If a nucleus undergoes beta decay (a neutron turns into a proton), what happens to the atomic number?",["It stays the same.","It increases by 1.","It decreases by 1.","The atom disappears."],"B",3,1,"907120c8286a"],["A teacher asks why Rutherford rejected Thomson's model. A student replies, \"Because some α-particles bounced back.\" Which assumption of Thomson's model was challenged?",["Presence of electrons","Uniform distribution of positive charge","Existence of isotopes","Presence of neutrons"],"B",3,1,"69dfb92e1162"],["A student claims that atoms of the same element must have identical masses. Which discovery proves the student wrong?",["Electron","Energy levels","Proton","Isotopes"],"D",3,1,"4fd4dd5183fa"],["A neutral atom has an electronic configuration 2,8,7. If it gains one electron, the resulting particle will:",["Lose a proton.","Become negatively charged.","Become neutral.","Become positively charged."],"B",3,0,"2787d20cca04"],["A particle contains 13 protons, 14 neutrons, and 10 electrons. Which statement is correct?",["Mass number = 24","Charge = –3","Atomic number = 14","Charge = +3"],"D",3,0,"7f790edb12b8"],["A student compares Rutherford's and Bohr's models. Which feature is present in both?",["Positive sphere with embedded electrons","Central nucleus","Neutrons in the nucleus","Quantized energy levels"],"B",3,1,"47b55ca89314"],["A student is asked which discovery directly contradicted Dalton's idea of indivisible atoms. Which answer is most appropriate?",["Discovery of the electron","Discovery of an isotope","Discovery of energy levels","Discovery of valency"],"A",3,1,"d7709e83ff78"],["A student notices that an electron jumps from the second shell to the fourth shell. According to Bohr's theory, the electron must have:",["Lost mass","Lost charge","Released energy","Absorbed energy"],"D",3,1,"d8ddab8db7ed"],["When an electron emits a photon, it must:",["Change into a proton.","Increase its velocity to infinity.","Move to a shell further from the nucleus.","Move to a shell closer to the nucleus."],"D",3,0,"33c51cff1c83"],["A student is comparing Cl-35 and Cl⁻. Which statement correctly distinguishes them?",["One differs in neutrons, the other differs in electrons.","Both differ in neutrons only.","Both differ in electrons only.","Both are isotopes."],"A",3,1,"bfd2124d8e2c"],["Which scientist discovered the neutron?",["Thomson","Chadwick","Rutherford","Dalton"],"B",3,0,"af15e1f36f60"],["Which scientist proposed the nuclear model of the atom after the gold-foil experiment?",["Dalton","Thomson","Rutherford","Bohr"],"C",3,0,"7b1344020967"],["An electron falls from the third shell back to the first shell. According to Bohr's theory, it:",["absorbs energy","releases energy","gains mass","loses its charge"],"B",3,1,"d8ddab8db7ed"],["In an electric field, a beam of neutrons:",["bends towards the positive plate","bends towards the negative plate","passes straight through","stops completely"],"C",3,0,"cfe19fb153ba"],["The mass of a proton is about the same as the mass of a:",["neutron","electron","photon","positron only"],"A",3,0,"44264e75721f"],["The number of protons in an atom is called its:",["mass number","atomic number","neutron number","valency"],"B",1,0,"46d1d0bfa7eb"],["Both Rutherford's and Bohr's atomic models include:",["electrons embedded in a positive sphere","a small, dense central nucleus","no electrons","atoms that cannot be divided"],"B",3,0,"47b55ca89314"],["A lithium atom has 3 protons and 4 neutrons. Its mass number is:",["4","7","3","1"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-nucleons"],["An atom of sodium has mass number 23 and atomic number 11. The number of neutrons in its nucleus is:",["34","23","11","12"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-nucleons"],["An atom of chlorine has mass number 35 and atomic number 17. The number of neutrons in its nucleus is:",["35","17","52","18"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-nucleons"],["An atom of silicon has mass number 28 and atomic number 14. The number of neutrons in its nucleus is:",["28","15","42","14"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-nucleons"],["An atom of boron has mass number 11 and atomic number 5. The number of neutrons in its nucleus is:",["11","5","6","16"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-nucleons"],["A carbon atom has 6 protons and 6 neutrons. Its mass number is:",["18","6","12","13"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-nucleons"],["The electronic configuration of an element with atomic number 17 is:",["2, 8, 8","2, 15","2, 8, 7","2, 8, 6"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-configuration"],["The electronic configuration of an element with atomic number 18 is:",["2, 16","2, 8, 8, 1","2, 8, 7","2, 8, 8"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-configuration"],["The electronic configuration of an element with atomic number 3 is:",["2, 2","2, 1","1, 2","2"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-configuration"],["The electronic configuration of an element with atomic number 13 is:",["2, 11","2, 8, 4","2, 8, 3","2, 8, 2"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-configuration"],["The electronic configuration of an element with atomic number 6 is:",["2, 5","2, 4","1, 5","2, 3"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-configuration"],["The electronic configuration of an element with atomic number 9 is:",["2, 7","2, 6","1, 8","2, 8"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-configuration"],["The atomic number of the element in the Na⁺ ion is 11. How many electrons does the ion have?",["12","11","10","9"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-ion-electrons"],["The atomic number of the element in the Al³⁺ ion is 13. How many electrons does the ion have?",["11","13","10","16"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-ion-electrons"],["The atomic number of the element in the N³⁻ ion is 7. How many electrons does the ion have?",["4","11","10","7"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-ion-electrons"],["The atomic number of the element in the Mg²⁺ ion is 12. How many electrons does the ion have?",["10","14","12","11"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-ion-electrons"],["The atomic number of the element in the Ca²⁺ ion is 20. How many electrons does the ion have?",["18","20","19","22"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-ion-electrons"],["The atomic number of the element in the O²⁻ ion is 8. How many electrons does the ion have?",["8","6","11","10"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-ion-electrons"],["Boron has atomic number 5. How many valence electrons does its atom have?",["3","5","4","2"],"A",1,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-valence-electrons"],["Magnesium has atomic number 12. How many valence electrons does its atom have?",["2","3","1","6"],"A",1,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-valence-electrons"],["Potassium has atomic number 19. How many valence electrons does its atom have?",["1","7","2","4"],"A",1,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-valence-electrons"],["Beryllium has atomic number 4. How many valence electrons does its atom have?",["1","6","3","2"],"D",1,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-valence-electrons"],["Calcium has atomic number 20. How many valence electrons does its atom have?",["4","3","6","2"],"D",1,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-valence-electrons"],["Nitrogen has atomic number 7. How many valence electrons does its atom have?",["6","5","3","2"],"B",1,0,"gen-chemistry-c3-valence-electrons"]]$j$::jsonb, true);
select public.prepify_load_bank('Chemistry', 4, $j$[["How is the modern periodic table arranged?",["By increasing atomic mass","By decreasing reactivity","By number of neutrons","By increasing the proton number"],"D",3,0,"bac1879e2769"],["Which block of the periodic table contains the transition metals?",["s-block","d-block","p-block","f-block"],"B",3,0,"f8c950d0c7aa"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2,8,1. It belongs to:",["Group 1","Group 18","Group 2","Group 17"],"A",3,0,"a2db521d09ec"],["Which element belongs to the same group as sodium?",["Magnesium","Aluminium","Potassium","Chlorine"],"C",3,0,"41698b9ade75"],["What is the common name for the elements in Group 17?",["Alkali metals","Noble gases","Alkaline earth metals","Halogens"],"D",3,0,"abd0ba8f1637"],["As you move from left to right across a period, the atomic radius generally:",["Increases","Decreases","Remains constant","Doubles"],"B",3,0,"cf44df4a51e4"],["Why do transition metals exhibit variable oxidation states?",["They have large atomic radii.","They have very few electrons.","Their s and d subshell electrons are close enough in energy to be used in bonding.","They are all magnetic."],"C",3,0,"03d024a93ad9"],["Which group of elements is known for having a full outermost electron shell, making them inert?",["Alkali metals","Halogens","Noble gases","Transition metals"],"C",3,0,"f9262c9c6c99"],["The \"shielding effect\" is most responsible for:",["Preventing neutron decay","Making the nucleus larger","Increasing electronegativity","The ease of losing electrons in lower group elements"],"D",3,0,"a95bf6a1f338"],["The trend for atomic radius down a group is an increase because:",["Proton number decreases","New electron shells are added","Electrons are lost","Electronegativity increases"],"B",3,0,"c7a503a1bbe8"],["Which element has the largest atomic radius?",["Li","Na","K","Rb"],"D",3,0,"69c70846de4a"],["Lanthanides and actinides belong to:",["s-block","p-block","d-block","f-block"],"D",3,0,"81425c7df873"],["What defines the \"period\" number of an element?",["The number of valence electrons","The number of electron shells","The number of neutrons","The atomic mass"],"B",3,0,"26ad303ad865"],["Which element has the highest electronegativity?",["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Fluorine","Chlorine"],"C",3,0,"fbdb8721f81e"],["Which element is expected to have the highest ionization energy?",["Li","Be","B","Ne"],"D",3,0,"46a99e325551"],["Which of these is a property of transition metals?",["They are all gases at room temperature","They form colored compounds","They are highly electronegative","They have very low melting points"],"B",3,0,"d6708ae94d82"],["What does the group number indicate about the valence shell?",["The number of inner shell electrons","The number of protons","The number of valence electrons","The number of neutrons"],"C",3,0,"b9cc050f7348"],["Two elements are in the same group of the periodic table. Which property is most likely to be similar?",["Number of valence electrons","Atomic mass","Number of shells","Atomic size"],"A",3,0,"b9e1e745fb9f"],["An element belongs to Period 3 and Group 16. How many occupied shells and valence electrons does it have?",["16 shells, 3 valence electrons","3 shells, 6 valence electrons","3 shells, 16 valence electrons","6 shells, 3 valence electrons"],"B",3,0,"65a1b926a53f"],["An element lies immediately to the right of sodium in the same period. Compared with sodium, it is expected to have:",["Fewer protons","More occupied shells","Higher ionization energy","Larger atomic radius"],"C",3,0,"8820f10910fa"],["A student compares fluorine and chlorine. Which property increases from fluorine to chlorine?",["Electron-attracting power","Electronegativity","Ionization energy","Atomic radius"],"D",3,1,"e67ef8e0b950"],["Which pair of elements is expected to show the greatest similarity in chemical behavior?",["Li and Na","Mg and Al","Na and Mg","Cl and Ar"],"A",3,0,"7caf42d4d282"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2,8,8. What can be inferred about its chemical activity?",["Highly reactive","Very unreactive","Moderately reactive","Reacts violently with water"],"B",3,0,"baad6d45cd9c"],["An element in Group 2 forms ions by:",["Gaining two electrons","Sharing eight electrons","Gaining one electron","Losing two electrons"],"D",3,0,"c3b3456a799e"],["The tendency of an atom to attract electrons in a bond is known to be highest for elements located:",["Lower left of the periodic table","Upper right of the periodic table","Middle of the periodic table only","In Group 1"],"B",3,0,"7ae3b1332e23"],["Which statement correctly compares sodium and chlorine?",["Both readily lose electrons","Both belong to the same group","Both have identical chemical properties","Sodium tends to lose electrons, while chlorine tends to gain electrons"],"D",3,0,"232287e8b345"],["An element lies at the extreme right of a period. Which property is most likely?",["Strong metallic character","Tendency to lose electrons easily","High stability due to a complete outer shell","Lowest ionization energy"],"C",3,0,"ab767ec9ead0"],["Why does the first ionization energy not strictly increase across Period 3, showing a slight dip between Magnesium and Aluminum?",["Aluminum has a larger atomic radius than Magnesium.","Aluminum's valence electron is in a p-subshell, which is slightly further from the nucleus and shielded by the s-subshell.","Magnesium has a higher nuclear charge than Aluminum.","Aluminum has more protons, which repel the valence electrons."],"B",3,0,"b706f3a3732a"],["Why is the ionic radius of an O²⁻ ion significantly larger than that of a Ne atom, even though they have the same number of electrons?",["Neon is a metal.","The oxygen nucleus is larger.","O²⁻ has fewer protons (8) pulling on the same number of electrons (10), resulting in less effective nuclear attraction.","They have the same nuclear charge."],"C",3,0,"ab46ab31fd30"],["Consider elements X and Y in the same period. If X is a metal and Y is a non-metal, which is more likely to form an acidic oxide?",["X","Y","Both are equally acidic.","Neither can form oxides."],"B",3,1,"3ace10f1af03"],["What is the correct order of increasing atomic radius for the isoelectronic series N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Ne, Na⁺?",["N³⁻<O²⁻<F⁻<Ne<Na⁺","Na⁺<Ne<F⁻<O²⁻<N³⁻","Ne<Na⁺<N³⁻<O²⁻<F⁻","N³⁻=O²⁻=F⁻=Ne=Na⁺"],"B",3,0,"b356ec5ca9c3"],["Why is the ionic radius of a cation smaller than that of its neutral atom?",["Cations lose an electron shell, and remaining electrons experience a higher effective nuclear charge.","Cations gain more protons.","Electrons are heavier than protons.","The nucleus disappears."],"A",3,0,"9038dd4f0800"],["Which block of the periodic table represents elements whose valence electrons are filling orbitals that are NOT the outermost shell?",["s-block","p-block","d-block","All of the above."],"C",3,0,"e280f444784b"],["Which factor causes the decrease in electron affinity from Chlorine to Bromine?",["Bromine is a metal.","Loss of s-electrons.","Decreased nuclear charge.","Increased electron-electron repulsion in the larger 4p orbital."],"D",3,0,"cb38cbe4642a"],["Why are noble gases not truly \"inert\"?",["Because they have no electrons.","Because larger ones (like Xenon) can be forced to react with highly electronegative elements like Fluorine.","Because they are all radioactive.","Because they are actually metals."],"B",3,0,"221aeafb7b91"],["Why does the atomic radius of transition metals show a \"dip\" in the middle of the period?",["Because protons are lost.","Because they become noble gases.","Because of the change in metallic bonding character as d-orbitals become half-filled.","Because they change into liquids."],"C",3,0,"25b9bd706d74"],["An element belongs to Group 17. Which statement CANNOT be true?",["It contains seven valence electrons.","It forms a negative ion.","It is chemically inert.","It has a strong tendency to gain electrons."],"C",3,0,"6c96674e46cc"],["The trend in boiling points for Group 17 (Halogens) is:",["Increasing down the group due to increased Van der Waals forces.","Decreasing down the group.","Constant.","Random."],"A",3,0,"77752c255a92"],["What does a negative value for electron affinity mean?",["The process is endothermic.","The process is exothermic.","The atom is a metal.","The atom is unstable."],"B",3,0,"2a94af3273dc"],["Which property measures the \"energy cost\" of adding an electron?",["Electronegativity","Ionization energy","Atomic radius","Electron affinity"],"D",3,1,"4e1ca2c96117"],["What is the relationship between atomic number and periodicity?",["Periodicity is a function of the repetition of electronic configuration.","It is completely random.","It depends only on mass.","Protons define the color of the element."],"A",3,0,"69bcda99e9ae"],["What differentiates the actinides from the lanthanides?",["Actinides are all non-metals.","Lanthanides are larger.","Actinides have no electrons.","Lanthanides fill the 4f subshell, while actinides fill the 5f."],"D",3,0,"e740df08420c"],["Which of these sets represents an isoelectronic series where size is purely determined by proton count?",["F⁻,Na⁺,Mg²⁺","Li,Be,B","Cl,Ar,K","O²⁻,S²⁻,Se²⁻"],"A",3,0,"72f819b94ee5"],["In the Periodic Table, why is Helium often grouped with s-block elements in some theoretical models?",["It is very dense.","It is a metal.","Its electronic configuration (1s²) matches the s-block filling pattern.","It reacts with oxygen."],"C",3,0,"315f75660d05"],["The \"Diagonal Relationship\" between Lithium and Magnesium implies they share similar:",["Atomic mass.","Melting points.","Charge density and polarization power.","Number of shells."],"C",3,0,"78c19a06e3cc"],["Why is atomic radius a \"trend\" rather than an absolute property for all elements in a period?",["Because the periodic table is a diagram.","Because neutrons change the size.","Because atoms are always moving.","Because of varying metallic bonding strengths and orbital occupancy."],"D",3,0,"e99a8dd2e404"],["What is the most significant factor in the reactivity of Alkali metals?",["High density.","High electronegativity.","Low first ionization energy.","Full valence shells."],"C",3,0,"673b6844a9a4"],["What does a high electronegativity value indicate about an atom's behavior in a bond?",["It has a strong tendency to attract shared electrons towards itself.","It donates electrons.","It is a poor electron attractor.","It becomes radioactive."],"A",3,0,"3ca557260f50"],["Why does Carbon show unique bonding compared to other Group 14 elements?",["It is a metal.","It has a small atomic radius, allowing it to form strong multiple bonds (pi-bonds).","It is a noble gas.","It has no protons."],"B",3,0,"01494f8fbb4c"],["Which period contains the greatest number of elements?",["Period 1","Period 7","Period 3","Period 2"],"B",3,0,"ecbd4e3a207d"],["What trend is observed in the boiling points of Alkali metals down the group?",["They increase.","They stay constant.","They decrease as the metallic bonding becomes weaker due to larger atomic size.","They are random."],"C",3,1,"9c656422ac64"],["Which is the most reactive metal in the periodic table?",["Li","Na","Mg","Cs"],"D",3,0,"2cac0c7c76b9"],["Moving across a period, metallic character decreases mainly because atoms:",["Hold valence electrons more strongly","Lose protons","Become larger","Gain shells"],"A",3,0,"d29bc2c1c852"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8. It is expected to be:",["highly reactive","very unreactive","a soft metal","a strong reducing agent"],"B",3,0,"baad6d45cd9c"],["Which element belongs to the same group as chlorine?",["Sulphur","Argon","Bromine","Sodium"],"C",3,0,"41698b9ade75"],["Which element belongs to the same group as magnesium?",["Sodium","Calcium","Aluminium","Silicon"],"B",3,0,"41698b9ade75"],["Copper sulphate is blue and iron(III) chloride is yellow-brown. Coloured compounds like these are typical of:",["alkali metals","noble gases","transition metals","halogens"],"C",3,0,"d6708ae94d82"],["Alkali metals react vigorously with water mainly because they:",["have high density","lose their single valence electron easily","have full valence shells","are non-metals"],"B",3,0,"673b6844a9a4"],["Going down Group 1 from lithium to potassium, reactivity:",["decreases","increases","stays the same","becomes zero"],"B",3,0,"2cac0c7c76b9"],["From left to right across a period, metallic character:",["increases","decreases","stays the same","first rises then falls sharply"],"B",3,0,"d29bc2c1c852"],["Which period has only two elements?",["Period 1","Period 2","Period 3","Period 7"],"A",5,0,"ecbd4e3a207d"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2, 3. Its position in the periodic table is:",["Period 3, Group 2","Period 3, Group 13","Period 2, Group 14","Period 2, Group 13"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c4-period-group"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2, 1. Its position in the periodic table is:",["Period 2, Group 2","Period 2, Group 1","Period 1, Group 2","Period 3, Group 1"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c4-period-group"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2, 5. Its position in the periodic table is:",["Period 2, Group 16","Period 3, Group 15","Period 2, Group 15","Period 5, Group 2"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c4-period-group"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2, 7. Its position in the periodic table is:",["Period 3, Group 17","Period 2, Group 17","Period 2, Group 18","Period 7, Group 2"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c4-period-group"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 8, 1. Its position in the periodic table is:",["Period 4, Group 2","Period 4, Group 1","Period 1, Group 4","Period 5, Group 1"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c4-period-group"],["An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 4. Its position in the periodic table is:",["Period 4, Group 14","Period 4, Group 3","Period 3, Group 14","Period 3, Group 15"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c4-period-group"]]$j$::jsonb, true);
